    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "I'm currently reviewing the stability profiles for a new low-fat mayonnaise formulation. We're seeing some issues with phase separation in high-acid environments (pH < 3.8). Can you explain the specific molecular role of lecithin in stabilizing the oil-water interface under these high proton concentrations, and how the amphiphilic nature of the molecule helps prevent coalescence?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Lecithin, with its amphiphilic structure, contains both hydrophilic (water-attracting) phosphate head and hydrophobic (water-repelling) fatty acid chains. In an acidic mayonnaise formulation, the phosphate group can bond with water molecules, while the fatty acid chains interact with the oil phase. These interactions at the oil-water interface help prevent the merge of oil droplets. Lecithin's dual regions mediate a robust interfacial film that can withstand the stress caused by an acidic environment, inhibiting phase separation and maintaining a stable, emulsion consistency.",
      "source": "target",
